  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cloudy and drier today with an increasing chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms late tonight into Thursday morning. \n\nExpecting a mainly dry but cloudy day today as a 1026 mb surface \nhigh builds over the Canadian Maritimes and extends SE toward s \nsouthern New England. Cloud cover will keep high temperatures in the \nupper 60s to low 70s for most locations. Onshore flow means that \ncoastal areas likely see the coolest temps while the warmest \nconditions will be found across the interior. The day ends on an \nunsettled note as a frontal wave approaches from the Great Lakes. At \nthe surface, a warm front approaches southern New England overnight \nbringing the return of a moisture-rich airmass to the region. \nPrecipitable water values rise to 1-1.8\" overnight. Lift provided by \nthe jet positioned to the west and warm front will support the \nreturn of showers and perhaps some embedded, mostly elevated storms.\n\nShowers/storms more likely arrive in west and central portions of \nsouthern New England 11 PM to 2 AM arriving in the I-95 corridor 4 \nAM- 7 AM. There are indications of marginal elevated instability \nthat accompanies the plume of richer moisture. While instability \nlooks to be marginal (MLCAPE 400-700 J/kg), a strengthening low \nlevel jet on the order of 35-45 kts helps to increase the low-level \nwind field. Thus, any storms that form to the west of our CWA likely \ncontinue as they head east despite lower instability levels. \n\nSteering flow appears strong enough to support quicker storm motion \nwhich would limit the risk for flash flooding. If there are embedded \nstorms and downpours, can't rule out the potential for poor \ndrainage/urban flooding in the more vulnerable spots. As a result, \nthere is a marginal (level 1 of 4) risk for excessive rainfall and \nlocalized flash flooding.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain continues Thursday with a chance for a strong \nthunderstorm or two during the afternoon. \n\nYet another shortwave impulse dives S from eastern Canada Thursday. \nAn associated surface cold front provides lift for showers and \nthunderstorms by the afternoon The main question mark is on if \nstorms could become strong in this timeframe. Although the airmass \nis plenty humid and mid and upper level wind fields are seasonably \nstrong, considerable cloud cover could temper how much \ndestabilization occurs. GFS-based CAPE profiles show about 800-1600 \nJ/kg of most-unstable CAPE with moist-adiabatic mid-level lapse \nrates also tempering instability. It's still debatable if storms \ncould become strong but the chances seem low with more tempered \ninstability favored.  Still, Thursday looks to be a washout with \ngood chances for rains during the daytime hours, with more \nsubstantial clearing taking place Thursday evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Mix of clouds and sun with diurnally driven spot \nshowers Friday into the early part of Labor Day Weekend, but drier \nweather most of the time. Dry and seasonable Sunday and on the \nMonday holiday, with onshore breezes.\n\nStill under cyclonic flow aloft for Friday into the early part of \nLabor Day Weekend. This will keep weather pattern somewhat unsettled \nwith a mix of clouds and sun. Spot showers are possible Friday and \nSaturday afternoon but drier weather prevails most of the time. much \nof the time. Broad high pressure then works in from the north Sunday \ninto Labor Day, leading to onshore breezes, but with partly sunny \nskies and dry conditions.",
